{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/27qr01","slideIndex":0,"text":"Start at S with distance 0 and all other distances infinite. Relax the edges in the shown order: S→A gives A=4; S→B initially gives B=5; A→B improves B to 2; A→C gives C=8; B→C improves C to 5. A second pass makes no changes, so the algorithm stops early. In general, Bellman–Ford runs at most V−1 passes, then scans once more; any further improvement indicates a negative-weight cycle reachable from the source."}